106 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 88 0 R
/T (INTEGRATED 6-BAR KINEMATIC HARVESTING TOOL WITH GRAPPLING MECHANISM FOR EFFICIENT PEDICLE CUTTING)
/E (INTEGRATED 6-BAR KINEMATIC HARVESTING TOOL WITH GRAPPLING MECHANISM FOR EFFICIENT PEDICLE CUTTING)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
107 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (ABSTRACT )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
108 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (The present disclosure relates to fruits and vegetable harvesting \(like cherry tomatoes\), via an integrated scissor design interconnected with a spatial or planar mechanism that can hook, pull or both the plant pedicel. The entire kinematic chain is one degree of freedom and does not need multiple modes of actuation. During the operation, when the scissor blade starts to clamp together, the interconnected grappling mechanism hooks the pedicel of the fruit and pulls it towards the cutting area and might lock it in that region until the blades of scissor close and cut the pedicel. Usage of slots, springs, cam profiles, wedges can be done to optimize the cutting and grappling mechanism.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
109 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (BACKGROUND OF INVENTION)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
110 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E ([0002]   Manual harvesting of fruits and vegetables is very labor intensive and creates a challenge in Large scale production. Autonomous Harvesting of fruits and veggies have been extensively researched to solve the issues, but still commercial Robots for harvesting intricate yields such as tomatoes still remain a challenge [1]. A major concern is also due to inadequate end effectors which usually are not very accurate.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
111 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E ([0003] Harvesting of delicate yields requires an intricate process of physical manipulation to the target fruit region and extracting either by gripping the fruit itself or cutting the fruit bearing pedicel. [2] The integrated scissor mechanism discussed here is based upon the cutting of the pedicel and is non invasive to the fruit. Autonomous systems that have been deployed for this task usually rely on visual data from cameras. The visual data provides an approximate position of the pedicel and often has discrepancies. This can result in incomplete cuts or damage to the fruit itself. To avoid this, we need a hooking mechanism, best if coupled to the scissor/cutter, which can pull out the pedicel from a zone of possibilities of occurrence as per the detected cutting point, and bring it into the correct region for cutting. Existing end effector focusing on cutting of pedicel, [1, 10-12] lacks the ability of grappling it, thus there is a need for this invention which can perform the aforesaid. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
112 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E ([0005]  There has been significant advancement in the field of robotic harvesting. Lots of different types of robot end effector have been conceptualized which can grasp, clamp, suck or cut the fruit off the plant and none come close to the design presented by us. In the context of the grasping mechanisms we have end effectors like [3], which contain three finger-like soft projections which surround the fruit, grasp it, and rotate it out of the peduncle. In [4] it contains two finger-like clampers to grip and twist in a similar way. In case of [5] The end-effector was designed with four fingers that have foam sponge pads with motion controlled by solenoid activation. The has also been development of novel flat grippers [6], but the usage of grippers in general do cause damage to the fruit itself and can be challenging specially operating in a cluster of fruits or vegetables. Other studies also displayed various innovative ideas such as the usage of scissor mechanisms coupled together to form a retractable closed cylinder or sphere which can surround the fruit and cut it [7] or the usage of suction force to separate the fruit from its clusters and later cut it [8].)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
113 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E ([0006] Various cutting mechanisms have been developed for harvesting of hanging fruits and vegetables but they have been mainly focused on optimizing the cutting by addition of clampers to the main body of the commercially available scissors [9], springs and rubber bearings mounted on scissor with fixed and dynamic fingers [10] or improvement of blade structure and its controlling mechanism with the help of a driving cylinder [11]. Usage of a hook as a support mechanism has also been studied like in the case of [1] where the hook/supporter present at the scissor handle is pulled in and pushed out by the usage of a string and a spring and via kinematic linkages. The supporter itself cannot reach out to hook out any pedicle but is present to aid during cutting. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
114 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (DESCRIPTION OF THE INVENTION )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
115 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E ([0001]   1. Field of the Invention )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
116 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E ([0004]  2. Prior Arts )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
117 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E ([0007]  The hook mechanism presented in [12] needs multiple modes of actuation and the hooking assembly uses a lot of space around the peduncle, thus making it very difficult to cut out fruits individually in a bunch. Thereafter, Having a specialized hook as presented in which is kinematically coupled to the cutting mechanism we can have major advantages such as pulling out small fruits from a cluster, optimizing the cutting space, avoiding to cause harm to nearby fruits, higher  accuracy and precision in cutting.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
118 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E ([0008]  Based on the above reasons, the primary objective of the present invention is to provide a mechanism fulfilling the effective cutting of a fruit pedicle during autonomous harvesting. As shown in Figure 1\(a\) and Figure 1\(b\) This Kinematic pedicel cutter provided by the present invention is able to transmit the motion generated by two main arms of the mechanism \(1 & 2\), housing the blades \(11 & 21\), to the end of the hooking linkage \(6\).  The whole process is carried out by a single degree of freedom without the requirement of any other modes of actuation for the hook. The main arms of the scissor mechanism can be actuated by an external gripper actuator and its motion and force is transmitted to all the bars of the mechanism. Moreover, as  shown in Figure 1\(b\), the mechanism contains the initiation bar \(3\), supporting bar \(4\), Handling bar \(5\). The end point of the hook \(6\) has to follow a predetermined trajectory for the most effective method of harvesting the fruits or vegetables. This method initiates at hooking the fruit pedicle at the right orientation and then pulling it out apart from its bunch. This isolates the fruit pedicle and thus makes it easier to cut out the fruit without causing any harm to the neighboring plant. After cutting the fruit the hook can reopen with the same trajectory in reverse direction to cause minimal disturbance to the plant. This predictive operation of the integrated kinematic cutter leads us to define a definite workspace through which efficient cutting can be provided. Furthermore, the mechanism presented also fulfills the criteria of the appropriate timing and velocity of the hook end point with respect to the movement of the cutter blades so as to perform the entire cutting motion accurately. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
