/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E ([0009]   According to the embodiment of the present invention, to coordinate with the cutting of the blades, the motion of the kinematic mechanism is timed accordingly. The entire motion of hooking and pulling is in sync with the blades reaching the cutting position. The entire motion of the hook can be divided into 3 different time phases with their own specific speeds so as to insure a guaranteed cut. The time phases are i\) Hooking phase as shown in between Figure 2\(a\) and Figure 2\(b\), ii\) Pulling phase as shown in between Figure 2\(b\) and Figure 2\(c\), iii\) holding phase as shown in between Figure 2\(b\) and Figure 2\(c\). During the Hooking phase in the motion of the mechanism, the hook end moves at a very high velocity. This is made to ensure that the pedicle is hooked quickly as soon as the cutting blades begin to actuate towards each other. During this phase the rotational velocity of the hook is much higher in magnitude than the translational )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [49]
>>
endobj
156 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (WORKING OF THE INTEGRATED KINEMATIC CUTTER)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [50]
>>
endobj
157 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 88 0 R
/Pg 10 0 R
/A [1113 0 R 0]
/K [51]
>>
endobj
158 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (velocity to facilitate proper hooking motion. The next phase of the hook motion is the Pulling phase. It is the longest phase of the entire motion. During this phase the pedicle of the fruit is pulled inside the main cutting area with a good trade-off between the pulling speed and the pulling force. During the end of the pulling phase the speed of the hook further slows down and enters into the holding phase. The cutting of the fruit pedicle is usually observed during the onset of the holding phase. The holding phase is the most stable state of the hook with very little motion. During this phase the pedicle more or less gets locked into the most apt zone of the cutting blades because of the approximately static state of the hook. Thus this entire is observed. The combination of the three phases result in one of the most optimum cutting motion phrasal movement of the hook in correspondence to cutter blades results in highly accurate and efficient cutting.  )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
159 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E ([0010]   According to the embodiment of the present invention, the integrated kinematic cutting mechanism is one degree of freedom and a generally 6 bar mechanism performing the entire harvesting motion. The mechanism can also be an even bar mechanism above a 6 bar mechanism like a 8 bar mechanism or 10 bar mechanism or higher which can perform the same task with higher complexity in motion. With increase in the the number of kinematic linkages of the mechanism, results in an increment of subparts of the end hook. These additional subparts are called the intermediate bars which can form a serial chain)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
160 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (ending with the increase in sub-partition of the hook, the hooking motion is achieved by proficient curling of the intermediate bar\(s\) and the end hook around the fruit pedicel. Incase of 6 bar the curling motion is replaced by curved hook, which results in similar outcome as per higher bar mechanisms where curling is observed. Moreover in an 8 bar or higher cutting kinematic mechanism, the backlash from the fruit pedicle also disturbs the curled hooking shape, thus hindering the pulling force.  )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
161 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E ([0011] According to the embodiment of the present invention, as per the general case of the 6-bar mechanism, all the bars perform their respective roles to contribute to the final trajectory of the hook. As shown in Figure 1. the bars 1 to 6 make up the integrated kinematic mechanism with its kinematic representation)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
