/E ([0012]   According to the embodiment of the present invention, As per the previous explanation of the 6 bar version as shown in Figure 4 \(a\) - \(c\), the motion of 8 bar and higher cutting mechanism can also be analyzed. In all these cases we assume the facts that, i\) we generate the pulling inside motion of the hook end via the handling bar \(Bar 5 in case of previously explained 6 bar mechanism\) and ii\) the hook is linked to n number of intermediate bars \(n=1 in case of 8 bar mechanism\). iii\) The n number of intermediate bars gain their motion via connecting bars pivoted to various links of the mechanism \(In case of 8 bar mechanism presented we pivoted the connecting bar to end of the initiation bar {equivalent to bar 3 of the previously explained 6 bar mechanism}\). So as explained previously with case 6 bar, similarity here in the case of higher bars, as the two arms of the cutter actuate towards each other, they rotate the initiating bar which in turn pulls down the supporting bar \(equivalent to bar 4 of the 6 bar mechanism\). The supporting bar transmits its motion to the handling bar, which controls the motion of the hook end. The intermediate bars follow suit, following the trail led by the hook end via the pivots points of the connecting bars. Thus, the hook-end followed by the connecting bars form the basis of the grappling motion of the fruit pedicel. With the increase in the number of bars we can increase the number of time phrases that can be generated and optimize it by adjusting various physical parameters of the bars. This general working principle sums up the entire set of mechanisms that can be generated successfully and thus by tweaking the various parameters such as lengths, size, angles or number of bars, we can generate various sets of cutters suitable for vast operations. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [54]
>>
endobj
