/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 90 0 R
/E ([0015]   According to the embodiment of the present invention, during the final holding phase, the position of the hook is maintained at an approximating static state so as to lock the pedicel in the most appropriate position for cutting. This state is achieved because of the handling bar which tries to keep the vertical position of revolute joint point F from the origin O, almost constant during the holding phase. If we consider a vertical axis from point F called handling axis, during the onset of the holding phase, the handling bar is inclined about 15 degrees to the right side of the handling axis, as seen from front view. After this any actuation provided to the mechanism will result primarily in the rotation of the holding bar about point F which is approximately fixated. Thus only the inclination of the holding bar changes from right to left side of the holding axis. This continues until the hook inclination has switched it direction to the left side of the holding axis, with a maximum inclination of 10 degrees. After this the mechanism geometrically constrained as it reached its maximum closing state.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [21]
>>
endobj
255 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 90 0 R
/E ([0016]   According to the embodiment of the present invention, the kinematic cutting mechanism has to be properly oriented when it is used for harvesting. This orientation must be facilitated by use of various robotic systems, automotives, or machineries. The invention presented here, is usually actuated by a set of industrial grippers who opens and closes the main arms of the mechanism thus providing appropriate velocity and force to the system. The gripper can be mounted on industrial robots such as a serial arm which can reach and orient the cutter to best alignment for cutting the pedicels. The arm can be mounted on any vehicle/rail that travel to the various parts of the farm. We can also implement various detection and approaching mechanisms that can assist in reaching the pedicel. One such architecture is is presented in the flow chart Fig. 13, where we use point cloud data and control systems to harvest the fruits. According to the embodiment of the present invention, the invention can also have cam slots so as to ensure the smooth operation of the revolute joints without any misalignment. The overlapping bars of the mechanism need to be placed with clearances so as to prevent any collision during operation.  All the learances also need to account for the proximity of the cutting blades. Usage of thinner bars of in mechanism could help in bringing the hook closer to the cutting blades and thus increasing its capability to hook smaller pedicles. But using thinner bars would compromise with the strength and pulling force of the mechanism. Usage of bearing in some of the revolute joints can facilitate smooth rotation and handle unexpected loads. Usage of compression / extension springs can boost the overall robustness of the system. The blade and hook geometry also play a very crucial role in determining the outcome of the harvest. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [22]
>>
endobj
